Write an archive's entries to a new archive in another format, or with other options: zip,
bhive, 7z, tar, cpio, ar, cab, ISO, squashfs or DMG, tar and cpio under a codec too, from any
archive
BeeZip reads (RAR, packages, disk images and split archives included). The source isn't changed.
Entries are copied without decoding when the formats
match and no option asks for something else (`a.tar.gz` → `a.tar.zst` keeps every tar
entry, encoding the stream again). An encrypted source needs `new_password` or `decrypt`:
encryption is never dropped silently. What the new format can't hold is left out and
reported in `skipped`; hard links become files where the format has none.

Usage: beezip convert [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE> <OUTPUT> [ENTRIES]...

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive to convert

  <OUTPUT>
          The archive to write

  [ENTRIES]...
          Entries to convert (a folder takes everything under it). All when none are given

Options:
      --glob <PATTERN>
          Only entries matching these patterns

      --exclude <PATTERN>
          Leave out entries matching these patterns

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          The new archive's format [default: from the output's extension]
          
          [possible values: zip, bhive, 7z, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz, tar.lzma, tar.lz, tar.zst,
          tar.lz4, tar.br, tar.sz, tar.Z, tar.lzo, cpio, cpio.gz, cpio.bz2, cpio.xz, cpio.lzma,
          cpio.lz, cpio.zst, cpio.lz4, cpio.br, cpio.sz, cpio.Z, cpio.lzo, ar, cab, iso, squashfs,
          dmg]

  -m, --method <METHOD>
          The compression method, as for `archive.create`
          
          [possible values: store, deflate, bzip2, zstd, xz, ppmd, lzma2, lzma, copy, mszip, gzip,
          lz4, lzo]

  -l, --level <LEVEL>
          The compression level; `formats.list` shows each codec's and method's range. A .bhive's is
          0 to 4, or its name: `none`, `fast`, `balanced` (the default), `high` or `max`

      --threads <THREADS>
          Compression threads for codecs that have them; 0 is one per core [default: from config]

  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The source's password, if it is encrypted

      --new-password <NEW_PASSWORD>
          Encrypt the new archive with this password (zip, 7z)

      --encryption <ENCRYPTION>
          How to encrypt a new zip [default: aes256]. Zip only

          Possible values:
          - aes256:    AES-256, the default
          - zipcrypto: The legacy scheme, for tools such as Windows Explorer that can't read AES.
            Weak

      --encrypt-headers[=<ENCRYPT_HEADERS>]
          Encrypt the new 7z's names too [default: true with a password]. 7z only
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --decrypt[=<DECRYPT>]
          Write an encrypted source's entries in the clear
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --solid[=<SOLID>]
          Compress files together in solid blocks [default: true]. 7z only
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -v, --volume-size <SIZE>
          Split the new archive into volumes of this size: `<output>.001`, `.002`…

      --overwrite[=<OVERWRITE>]
          Replace the output if it exists
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --name-encoding <ENCODING>
          How the source's names stored as bytes are read [default: from config, `auto`]
          
          [possible values: auto, utf-8, cp437, cp850, cp852, cp866, cp874, cp932, cp936, cp949,
          cp950, cp1250, cp1251, cp1252, cp1253, cp1254, cp1255, cp1256, cp1257, cp1258, koi8-r,
          koi8-u, iso-8859-1, iso-8859-2, iso-8859-5, iso-8859-7, euc-jp, gb18030]

      --recover[=<RECOVER>]
          Open a .bhive whose index is missing or damaged (a download cut short) from the records
          before its cells, with the entries they still hold
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --recovery-key[=<RECOVERY_KEY>]
          Make a recovery key that opens the archive, shown once in the output: 48 digits to keep
          somewhere safe. `.bhive` only
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --to <KEY>
          Encrypt to these public keys, so their owners open it with their private keys: `age1…`,
          `age1pq1…`, or a contact's name. `.bhive` only

      --to-cert <CERT>
          Encrypt to these certificates: a thumbprint in your stores, or a certificate file.
          `.bhive` only

      --this-pc[=<THIS_PC>]
          Let this PC open it, with a key in its TPM. Needs another way in that works elsewhere.
          `.bhive` on Windows
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --this-pc-pin <PIN>
          A PIN for the This PC key, which the TPM protects against guessing

      --security-key[=<SECURITY_KEY>]
          Let a security key open it: Windows asks for a touch. `.bhive` on Windows
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --windows-hello[=<WINDOWS_HELLO>]
          Let Windows Hello open it (a face, a fingerprint or its PIN). It works only on this PC, so
          it needs another way in that works elsewhere. `.bhive` on Windows
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --strength <STRENGTH>
          How hard a `.bhive`'s password makes guessing [default: standard]

          Possible values:
          - standard: Argon2id with 1 GiB and 2 passes: about 0.3 s on a fast desktop, 0.6 s on a
            laptop
          - strong:   2 GiB and 3 passes: about 0.8 s and 1.7 s. Needs 2 GiB free to open

      --sign <SIGNER>
          Sign it: your signing key by name, a certificate's thumbprint in your stores, or a
          certificate file with its key beside it. `.bhive` only

      --dry-run[=<DRY_RUN>]
          Show what would be written without writing anything
          
          [possible values: true, false]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -i, --identity <FILE>
          A key file that opens encrypted .bhive archives (age identities, PEM and PKCS #8 keys);
          repeatable

      --password-stdin
          Read the password or recovery key from the first line of stdin, where other programs can't
          see it as they can the command line

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
